From cafdaf84548cdd651cf9850922f6564b53a3a2fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 14:10:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] fix(http): separate the JSON body ceiling from the
 upload ceiling

RequestBodyLimitMiddleware applied one ceiling to every route. That ceiling
follows the Storage transfer limits (defaults published as ~1 GiB, hard max
2 GiB), and FastAPI/pydantic buffer a JSON body in full before any handler
runs -- so anyone who could reach /api/auth/login (CSRF-exempt, pre-auth)
could push a 1 GiB JSON body into each worker's memory.

- request_body_limit_service.request_body_limit_for(path): the upload ceiling
  applies only to LARGE_BODY_PATH_PREFIXES (multipart upload routes, and the
  chat/gateway JSON routes that legitimately carry inline base64 images for
  vision models, plus chat-session sync payloads); every other route gets the
  new MAX_JSON_BODY_BYTES (default 8 MiB, never above the upload ceiling).
- The published limit file is now read at most once per 5s and only when its
  mtime changed; previously every HTTP request did stat+read+json.loads.
- If the handler already sent http.response.start when the streamed body
  crosses the limit, the middleware no longer emits a second response start
  (a protocol error); it closes the request instead.
- tls_edge_service.render_nginx_config mirrors the tiers: 'location /' gets
  the JSON ceiling, a regex location for the large-body prefixes inherits the
  http-level upload ceiling. The http-level directive is unchanged so
  read_nginx_client_max_body_mb() and the edge sync keep working.
- .env.example documents MAX_JSON_BODY_BYTES and the new meaning of
  MAX_REQUEST_BODY_BYTES.

Tests: tests/test_bounded_io.py -- 5 MiB JSON to /api/auth/login -> 413 while
the same body passes /api/chat/completions and a multipart upload route; no
duplicate response start after the handler answered; published limit parsed
once per TTL window.

